WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.280
Hello, and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan, and today we're going to

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.200
be looking at the hydrogenation of lipids.

00:00:12.795 --> 00:00:17.105
Meaning that whenever we have
a really big fat, sometimes

00:00:17.105 --> 00:00:19.685
you'll have unsaturated fats.

00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:23.925
So this is something that occurs,
that occurs artificially when you

00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:26.675
kind of want to buy different things
that you find in the grocery store.

00:00:27.065 --> 00:00:29.335
If you read a lot of those different
labels, you'll see that it might

00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:31.304
say that it's hydrogenated on it.

00:00:31.565 --> 00:00:35.864
This is a reaction that occurs to
help extend the shelf life of a lot

00:00:35.864 --> 00:00:38.805
of the different things that you
commonly find in your grocery store.

00:00:39.444 --> 00:00:43.494
Um, this also helps create a lot of the
different things that we use on a regular

00:00:43.495 --> 00:00:45.444
basis for baking and things like that.

00:00:45.844 --> 00:00:50.489
So it transforms the fats so it gets rid
of, um, not having as many unsaturated

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:54.935
fats in your diet, but it gets rid of also
the possibility of having cholesterol and

00:00:54.935 --> 00:00:56.925
things like that in those fats as well.

00:00:57.425 --> 00:01:01.524
So there's kind of like a hit or miss,
like sure you have more saturated fats.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:06.230
But, this is something that extends
shelf life, um, in a lot of your foods.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.330
So what it is, just to go through
the definition, is hydrogenation

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.330
is when we take hydrogen gas and
we bubble it through a heated oil.

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:17.910
So if you have like an oil in your
stove, you heat it up really hot, what

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.450
they do is they bubble hydrogen through
it in the presence of a catalyst.

00:01:21.459 --> 00:01:24.500
Usually this is like, uh, usually
nickel, for example, being that it's

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.260
usually a little cheaper to use.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.770
So, when they do this in industry,
they will take an unsaturated fat,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.290
which is typically a liquid or
something that's like a little goopy,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:41.010
and when you add a hydrogen to it,
it's going to remove the double bonds

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.720
and turn them into single bonds.

00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:46.384
So if you kind of think back to my
previous videos on organic when it came

00:01:46.384 --> 00:01:51.374
to addition reactions It's like if we had
the double bond Which is like are my two

00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:56.315
parts of my hand here and we're going to
be opening up that double bond, right?

00:01:56.335 --> 00:02:00.339
We're opening up the doors Now,
when you're doing that, you're

00:02:00.339 --> 00:02:03.809
going to be adding hydrogen
to each of those little bits.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:04.259
Okay.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.049
So let's take a look at this reaction
and try to see what it's all about.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:13.829
All right.

00:02:13.829 --> 00:02:16.579
So with the reaction, what it's,
what we're going to have here is

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:20.109
we have an unsaturated fat and I
highlighted the double bonds here

00:02:20.109 --> 00:02:23.850
in green and what we're going to be
doing is adding hydrogen to them.

00:02:24.265 --> 00:02:29.644
So like I said before, it's as if we're
going to be opening up the double doors.

00:02:29.924 --> 00:02:34.294
So if I change one of these double
bonds to blue here, we're going to

00:02:34.304 --> 00:02:38.734
be opening it up just like you're
opening up a cabinet in your house.

00:02:39.285 --> 00:02:43.305
So if I were to do that, we're
going to open up that double

00:02:43.305 --> 00:02:45.314
bond on each of these structures.

00:02:45.945 --> 00:02:50.855
And as a result, we're going to be
adding hydrogen to each one, all right?

00:02:51.105 --> 00:02:54.704
So being that we have our hydrogen
under a nickel catalyst right here,

00:02:55.164 --> 00:02:57.675
this is the idea of the reaction.

00:02:58.195 --> 00:03:01.104
So, but before we do that,
we want to illustrate that

00:03:01.105 --> 00:03:02.915
this is a complete reaction.

00:03:03.354 --> 00:03:06.705
So what we're going to do is
we're going to copy and paste

00:03:06.715 --> 00:03:07.935
our structure here, right?

00:03:07.975 --> 00:03:10.775
And sadly, if you're using a
notebook, you got to write it twice.

00:03:11.095 --> 00:03:14.165
That's the hardest part, but write them
as condensed structures, that makes

00:03:14.165 --> 00:03:17.965
it a lot easier when doing lipids,
because geometric is a little long.

00:03:17.965 --> 00:03:23.065
Remember, these types of molecules
are generally 12 to 18 or more carbons

00:03:23.075 --> 00:03:27.654
long, they're very difficult to always
draw, so make it easy on yourself.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.619
So what we're going to do is we're
going to open up that double bond.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:36.140
So just to illustrate the first one,
right, we added hydrogen to each one.

00:03:36.709 --> 00:03:39.000
Now what can you generally do instead?

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:44.660
Well, you can just write H2
on each of these, just like

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.769
this, and erase your bond.

00:03:47.339 --> 00:03:50.970
Now is this kind of the
best way to do that though?

00:03:51.019 --> 00:03:52.459
This is a condensed structure.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.809
So we're adding hydrogen to each, but
keep in mind that these structures

00:03:58.829 --> 00:04:04.240
here are all about, and I realized
I forgot some twos here, is that we

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:09.049
have a condensed structure, meaning we
can use subscripts to minimize these.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.995
So what we will do is instead is kind
of count how many hydrogens there are.

00:04:15.995 --> 00:04:19.534
So we're kind of showing our work,
or how many carbons you have.

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:27.384
So in the middle here, we have one, two
carbons, and then another set of CH2s.

00:04:27.385 --> 00:04:29.104
So we have seven of them next to it.

00:04:29.745 --> 00:04:32.344
So we have a total of nine
CH2s they're going to make.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.330
Over on our left hand side,
we also have seven CH2s, so

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.330
we'll add them all together.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.750
So if we kind of count, right,
we have seven plus seven plus

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.189
two, so that's sixteen total.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:47.859
So what we are going to do is kind
of erase this all here in the middle,

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:57.470
and we are going to make it so that
our CH2s will now say sixteen, because

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.100
we're counting all of our carbons,
and then we'll move over our CH3s.

00:05:03.215 --> 00:05:06.015
So this would be our
final answer down here.

00:05:06.015 --> 00:05:10.724
So notice how by hydrogenating it, it
all gets nice and clumped together as

00:05:10.735 --> 00:05:16.015
one little nice little solid is kind of
how I like to think about that, right?

00:05:16.045 --> 00:05:21.895
Because when it's saturated, this
is going to be a solid as a result.

00:05:22.085 --> 00:05:27.895
Okay, so we have a saturated,
so those are all single bonds

00:05:28.765 --> 00:05:30.925
versus when we started, right?

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:35.305
There were double bonds
or unsaturated fat.

00:05:35.969 --> 00:05:39.479
And that's the whole idea, is that
you're opening up those double bonds.

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:43.280
And now this can be done not just with
hydrogenation, it can be done with

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:49.480
bromination, hydrohalogenation, any of the
addition reactions can be done with this.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:53.819
And if you need a little refresher on
addition reactions, go refer back to

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.460
my video on addition reactions too.

